              "h": "Binary Representation & Six Core Bitwise Operators (Lab 1&2 PDF pp. 1–2)",
              "pts": [
                "<b>Why Bit Manipulation? (Lab PDF p. 1)</b>: Used in <b>data compression</b> (encoding/decoding bitstreams), <b>Exclusive-OR (XOR) encryption</b>, and low-level time/space optimization. Recall 1 byte = 8 bits; e.g., $14 = 1\\cdot 2^3 + 1\\cdot 2^2 + 1\\cdot 2^1 + 0\\cdot 2^0 = (1110)_2$, and $20 = (10100)_2$.",
                "<b>The Six Bitwise Operators (Lab PDF pp. 1–2)</b>:<ul><li><b>1. Bitwise NOT (`~`)</b>: Unary one's complement that flips every bit ($0 \\leftrightarrow 1$). On 3-bit unsigned: $N = 5 = (101)_2 \\implies \\sim N = (010)_2 = 2$.</li><li><b>2. Bitwise AND (`&`)</b>: Outputs `1` iff <b>both</b> compared bits are `1`. For $A = 5 = (101)_2, B = 3 = (011)_2$: $A \\;\\&\\; B = (001)_2 = 1$.</li><li><b>3. Bitwise OR (`|`)</b>: Outputs `1` if <b>at least one</b> bit is `1`. $5 \\;|\\; 3 = (101)_2 \\;|\\; (011)_2 = (111)_2 = 7$.</li><li><b>4. Bitwise XOR (`^`)</b>: Outputs `1` iff the two compared bits are <b>different</b> ($0$ if both are $0$ or both are $1$). $5 \\;\\wedge\\; 3 = (101)_2 \\;\\wedge\\; (011)_2 = (110)_2 = 6$.</li><li><b>5. Left Shift (`<<`)</b>: `x << k` shifts bits left by $k$ positions and appends $0$s at the right—equivalent to <b>multiplying by $2^k$</b>! In particular, `1 << n` $= 2^n$ (`1<<1 = 2`, `1<<2 = 4`, `1<<3 = 8`, `1<<4 = 16`).</li><li><b>6. Right Shift (`>>`)</b>: `x >> k` shifts bits right by $k$ positions—equivalent to <b>integer division by $2^k$</b> (`4 >> 1 = 2`, `6 >> 1 = 3`, `5 >> 1 = 2`).</li></ul>"
              ],
