            {
              "h": "Circular Shifts (Bit Rotation) & Classic Bit Manipulation Algorithms (Lab 1&2 PDF pp. 2–4)",
              "pts": [
                "<b>Circular Right Shift & Circular Left Shift (Lab PDF pp. 2–3)</b>: Unlike standard shifts that discard shifted-out bits, a circular shift wraps them around to the opposite end by combining a shift in direction $d$ with a complementary shift by $(W - d)$ bits (where $W$ is the bit-width, e.g., $W = 6$ in the lab trace or $W = 32$ for a C `int`) using bitwise OR (`|`):<ul><li><b>Lab Trace on 6-bit $N = (101001)_2$ rotated by $d = 2$</b>:<ul><li><b>Circular Right Shift (`>> <<`)</b>: $(N \\gg 2) = 001010$, $(N \\ll (6-2)) = (N \\ll 4) = 010000 \\implies 001010 \\;|\\; 010000 = \\mathbf{(011010)_2}$.</li><li><b>Circular Left Shift (`<< >>`)</b>: $(N \\ll 2) = 100100$, $(N \\gg (6-2)) = (N \\gg 4) = 000010 \\implies 100100 \\;|\\; 000010 = \\mathbf{(100110)_2}$.</li></ul></li></ul>",
                "<b>Exercise 1: Check if $x$ is a Power of 2 in $O(1)$ Time (Lab PDF pp. 3–4)</b>:<ul><li><b>Naive Loop</b>: `while (x % 2 == 0) x /= 2; return (x == 1);` takes $O(\\log N)$ time.</li><li><b>$O(1)$ Bit Trick (`x && !(x & (x - 1))`)</b>: Subtracting $1$ from $x$ flips the <b>rightmost set bit `1` and all `0` bits to its right</b> (e.g., $4 = (100)_2, 3 = (011)_2 \\implies 4 \\& 3 = (000)_2$; $6 = (110)_2, 5 = (101)_2 \\implies 6 \\& 5 = (100)_2$). Thus `x & (x - 1)` clears the rightmost `1` bit of $x$. Since powers of $2$ have <i>exactly one</i> bit set in binary, `x & (x - 1) == 0` for all powers of $2$ (and `x &&` excludes $x = 0$)!</li></ul>",
                "<b>Exercise 2: Count the Number of `1`s in Binary (Brian Kernighan's Algorithm, Lab PDF p. 4)</b>:<ul><li>Repeatedly executing `n = n & (n - 1); count++;` while `n != 0` strips one set bit per iteration, running in $O(k)$ time where $k$ is the exact number of `1` bits!</li></ul>",
                "<b>Exercise 3: Check if the $i$-th Bit is Set (Lab PDF p. 4)</b>:<ul><li>Mask $N$ with `1 << i`: `if (N & (1 << i)) return true; else return false;` ($O(1)$ time).</li></ul>"
              ],
              "code": "// 1. Circular Right & Left Shifts on 32-bit int (Lab 1 PDF p. 3)\nint rightRotate(int n, int d) {\n    return (n >> d) | (n << (32 - d));\n}\nint leftRotate(int n, int d) {\n    return (n << d) | (n >> (32 - d));\n}\n\n// 2. O(1) Power-of-Two Check (Lab 1 PDF p. 4)\nbool isPowerOfTwo(int x) {\n    return (x && !(x & (x - 1)));\n}\n\n// 3. Count Number of 1s in Binary (Brian Kernighan's Popcount)\nint count_one(int n) {\n    int count = 0;\n    while (n) {\n        n = n & (n - 1);   // clears rightmost set 1-bit\n        count++;\n    }\n    return count;\n}\n\n// 4. Check if ith Bit is Set\nbool checkBit(int N, int i) {\n    return (N & (1 << i)) != 0;\n}"
            }
