              "In binary, $(x - 1)$ has the exact same upper bits as $x$, except the rightmost `1` bit of $x$ becomes `0` and all trailing `0` bits to its right become `1`. Therefore, `x & (x - 1)` clears the rightmost `1` bit of $x$ in $O(1)$ time.<br>• <b>(a) Power of 2</b>: A positive integer $x$ is a power of 2 iff its binary representation contains exactly one `1` bit. Thus `x && !(x & (x - 1))` returns `true` iff $x$ is a power of 2.<br>• <b>(b) Count 1-bits</b>: Looping `while (n) { n = n & (n - 1); count++; }` clears one `1` bit per iteration until $n = 0$, counting all set bits in $O(\\text{# of set bits})$ steps.",
