              "<b>Method 1 (Divide-and-Conquer Recurrence)</b>: A heap of size $n$ consists of two sub-heaps of size $n/2$ plus the root, which takes $O(\\lg n)$ time to `MAX-HEAPIFY` (`siftDown`) down the height of the tree. Thus $T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(\\lg n)$. Here $a=2, b=2 \\implies n^{\\log_2 2} = n^1 = n$, and $f(n) = O(\\lg n) = O(n^{1 - \\epsilon})$ for any $\\epsilon \\in (0, 1)$. By <b>Master Theorem Case 1</b>, $T(n) = \\mathbf{\\Theta(n)}$.<br><b>Method 2 (Summation over Tree Heights)</b>: There are at most $\\lceil n / 2^{h+1} \\rceil$ nodes of height $h$, and each takes $O(h)$ work: $\\sum_{h=0}^{\\lfloor \\lg n \\rfloor} \\frac{n}{2^{h+1}} O(h) = O\\left(n \\sum_{h=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{h}{2^h}\\right) = O(n \\cdot 2) = \\mathbf{O(n)}$.",
