        {
          "q": "<b>Q5 (5 points)</b>: Prove that <code>BUILD-MAX-HEAP</code> has time complexity of $O(n)$. <span class=\"muted small\">(Midterm Exam 2024 & Alternative Midterm 2024 (Retake — Dr. Basheer Youssef & Dr. Amin Allam))</span>",
          "a": "A heap of $n$ elements has at most $\\lceil n / 2^{h+1} \\rceil$ nodes at height $h$, and calling <code>MAX-HEAPIFY</code> on a node at height $h$ takes $O(h)$ time. Summing over all heights $h = 0 \\dots \\lfloor \\lg n \\rfloor$:<br>$$T(n) = \\sum_{h=0}^{\\lfloor \\lg n \\rfloor} \\left\\lceil \\frac{n}{2^{h+1}} \\right\\rceil O(h) = O\\left(n \\sum_{h=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{h}{2^h}\\right).$$<br>Since the arithmetico-geometric series $\\sum_{h=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{h}{2^h} = \\frac{1/2}{(1 - 1/2)^2} = 2$ converges to a constant, $T(n) = O(2n) = \\mathbf{O(n)}$. (Equivalently, top-down $T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(\\lg n) = O(n)$ by Master Theorem Case 1.)"
        },
        {
          "q": "<b>Qa (6 Marks)</b>: Write a top-down divide-and-conquer recursive C++ function that converts any array to a min-heap array in $O(n)$ time complexity. Trace your code on the array <code>[5, 7, 2, 1, 4, 3, 6]</code> by re-writing the array after each modification. <span class=\"muted small\">(Midterm Exam 2024 & Alternative Midterm 2024 (Retake — Dr. Basheer Youssef & Dr. Amin Allam))</span>",
          "a": "<pre>void siftDown(int A[], int n, int i) {\n  int smallest = i, l = 2*i + 1, r = 2*i + 2;\n  if (l &lt; n &amp;&amp; A[l] &lt; A[smallest]) smallest = l;\n  if (r &lt; n &amp;&amp; A[r] &lt; A[smallest]) smallest = r;\n  if (smallest != i) {\n    swap(A[i], A[smallest]);\n    siftDown(A, n, smallest);\n  }\n}\nvoid buildMinHeapDC(int A[], int n, int i = 0) {\n  if (i &gt;= n / 2) return;          // leaf node\n  buildMinHeapDC(A, n, 2*i + 1);   // conquer left subtree\n  buildMinHeapDC(A, n, 2*i + 2);   // conquer right subtree\n  siftDown(A, n, i);               // combine in O(height)\n}</pre><b>Step-by-Step Trace on <code>[5, 7, 2, 1, 4, 3, 6]</code></b>:<br>1. Call on left child <code>i=1</code> (value <code>7</code>, children <code>1</code> at idx 3 and <code>4</code> at idx 4): swap <code>7 ↔ 1</code> $\\rightarrow$ <code>[5, 1, 2, 7, 4, 3, 6]</code>.<br>2. Call on right child <code>i=2</code> (value <code>2</code>, children <code>3</code> at idx 5 and <code>6</code> at idx 6): <code>2 &le; 3, 6</code> (no swap).<br>3. Call <code>siftDown</code> on root <code>i=0</code> (value <code>5</code>, children <code>1</code> and <code>2</code>):<br>   • Swap <code>5 ↔ 1</code> $\\rightarrow$ <code>[1, 5, 2, 7, 4, 3, 6]</code>.<br>   • Continue <code>siftDown</code> on <code>5</code> at idx 1 (children <code>7</code> at idx 3 and <code>4</code> at idx 4): swap <code>5 ↔ 4</code> $\\rightarrow$ <b>Final Min-Heap: <code>[1, 4, 2, 7, 5, 3, 6]</code></b>."
        },
        {
          "q": "<b>Qb (6 Marks)</b>: Write a top-down divide-and-conquer recursive C++ function that finds all inversions of an array in $O(n \\log n)$ time. Trace your code on <code>[5, 7, 2, 1, 4, 3, 6, 8]</code> by writing the inversions in the order found. <span class=\"muted small\">(Midterm Exam 2024 & Alternative Midterm 2024 (Retake — Dr. Basheer Youssef & Dr. Amin Allam))</span>",
          "a": "<b>Inversions Found in Merge Order (11 Total)</b>:<br>1. Merging <code>[2]</code> and <code>[1]</code>: <b>(2, 1)</b><br>2. Merging <code>[5, 7]</code> and <code>[1, 2]</code>: <b>(5, 1), (7, 1), (5, 2), (7, 2)</b><br>3. Merging <code>[4]</code> and <code>[3]</code>: <b>(4, 3)</b><br>4. Merging <code>[1, 2, 5, 7]</code> and <code>[3, 4, 6, 8]</code>: <b>(5, 3), (7, 3), (5, 4), (7, 4), (7, 6)</b>.<br>Total = <b>11 inversions</b> found in $T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n) = O(n \\log n)$ time."
        },
        {
          "q": "<b>Qc (8 Marks)</b>: Write a greedy C++ function that computes the Huffman codes of all characters of a string in $O(n \\log n)$ time. Trace your code on <code>\"aabcaaadbcddaa\"</code> and prove the correctness of the algorithm. <span class=\"muted small\">(Midterm Exam 2024 & Alternative Midterm 2024 (Retake — Dr. Basheer Youssef & Dr. Amin Allam))</span>",
          "a": "<b>Trace on <code>\"aabcaaadbcddaa\"</code> (Length 14)</b>:<br>• Frequencies: <code>a: 7, d: 3, b: 2, c: 2</code>.<br>• Step 1: Merge <code>b(2) + c(2) = bc(4)</code>.<br>• Step 2: Merge <code>d(3) + bc(4) = dbc(7)</code>.<br>• Step 3: Merge <code>a(7) + dbc(7) = Root(14)</code>.<br>• <b>Codes</b>: <code>a = 0</code>, <code>d = 10</code>, <code>b = 110</code>, <code>c = 111</code> (25 bits total).<br><b>Correctness Proof</b>: (1) <i>Greedy-Choice Lemma</i>: By exchange argument, moving the two lowest-frequency symbols $x, y$ to the deepest sibling leaves of any optimal tree $T$ does not increase $B(T) = \\sum f(c)d_T(c)$ since $(f(a)-f(x))(d_T(a)-d_T(x)) \\ge 0$. (2) <i>Optimal Substructure Lemma</i>: Replacing $x, y$ by parent $z$ with $f(z)=f(x)+f(y)$ satisfies $B(T) = B(T') + f(x) + f(y)$, so an optimal tree for the reduced alphabet yields an optimal tree for the original alphabet."
        },
        {
          "q": "<b>Midterm 2021 Q1 (10 points)</b>: Assume you have hash table $T$ with size $= 10$. Add values <code>(0, 30, 1, 55, 16, 28, 13, 9, 26)</code> using: (1) Linear probing $h(k) = k \\bmod 10$; (2) Quadratic probing $h(k) = k \\bmod 10$; (3) Double hashing $h_1(k) = k \\bmod 10,\\; h_2(k) = 5 - (k \\bmod 5)$; (4) Chaining. <span class=\"muted small\">(Midterm Exams 2021 & 2022 (with Official Model Answers — Dr. Basheer Youssef))</span>",
          "a": "• <b>1. Linear Probing ($m=10$)</b>: <code>[0: 0, 1: 30, 2: 1, 3: 13, 4: -, 5: 55, 6: 16, 7: 26, 8: 28, 9: 9]</code>.<br>• <b>2. Quadratic Probing ($m=10$)</b>: <code>[0: 0, 1: 30, 2: 1, 3: 13, 4: -, 5: 55, 6: 16, 7: 26, 8: 28, 9: 9]</code>.<br>• <b>3. Double Hashing ($m=10, h_2(k)=5-(k\\bmod 5)$)</b>: <code>0→0; 30(h₁=0,h₂=5)→5; 1→1; 55(h₁=5,h₂=5)</code> probes $(5+5i)\\bmod 10 \\in \\{5, 0\\}$ (both full!) $\\implies$ <b>55 CANNOT BE INSERTED!</b> Remaining keys give <code>[0: 0, 1: 1, 2: -, 3: 13, 4: 26, 5: 30, 6: 16, 7: -, 8: 28, 9: 9]</code>.<br>• <b>4. Chaining ($m=10$)</b>: <code>0: [0, 30], 1: [1], 3: [13], 5: [55], 6: [16, 26], 8: [28], 9: [9]</code>."
        },
        {
          "q": "<b>Midterm 2022 Q1 (6 points)</b>: Assume you have hash table $T$ with size $= 11$. Add values <code>(0, 30, 1, 55, 16, 28, 13, 9, 26)</code> using: (1) Linear probing $h(k) = k \\bmod 11$; (2) Quadratic probing $h(k) = k \\bmod 11$; (3) Double hashing $h_1(k) = k \\bmod 11,\\; h_2(k) = 7 - (k \\bmod 7)$; (4) Chaining. <span class=\"muted small\">(Midterm Exams 2021 & 2022 (with Official Model Answers — Dr. Basheer Youssef))</span>",
          "a": "Initial mod 11 values: <code>0→0, 30→8, 1→1, 55→0, 16→5, 28→6, 13→2, 9→9, 26→4</code>.<br>• <b>1. Linear Probing ($m=11$)</b>: <code>[0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 55, 3: 13, 4: 26, 5: 16, 6: 28, 7: -, 8: 30, 9: 9, 10: -]</code>.<br>• <b>2. Quadratic Probing ($m=11$)</b>: <code>55</code> ($h=0$, $0+1^2=1$ full, $0+2^2=4$) $\\to 4$; <code>26</code> ($h=4$, $4+1^2=5$ full, $4+2^2=8$ full, $4+3^2=13\\equiv 2$ full, $4+4^2=20\\equiv 9$ full, $4+5^2=29\\equiv 7$) $\\to 7$. Result: <code>[0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 13, 3: -, 4: 55, 5: 16, 6: 28, 7: 26, 8: 30, 9: 9, 10: -]</code>.<br>• <b>3. Double Hashing ($m=11$)</b>: <code>55</code> ($h_1=0, h_2=7-(55\\bmod 7)=1$): probes $0+1=1$ (full), $0+2=2$ $\\to$ lands at <code>2</code>! Result: <code>[0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 55, 3: 13, 4: 26, 5: 16, 6: 28, 7: -, 8: 30, 9: 9, 10: -]</code>.<br>• <b>4. Chaining ($m=11$)</b>: <code>0: [0, 55], 1: [1], 2: [13], 4: [26], 5: [16], 6: [28], 8: [30], 9: [9]</code>."
        },
        {
          "q": "<b>Midterm 2016 Q3 / 2017 Q5 / 2018 Q3</b>: For each algorithm listed below, state its worst-case running time: (1) Binary Search, (2) Insertion Sort, (3) Merge Sort, (4) Counting Sort, (5) Heap Sort, (6) Red-Black Tree operations. <span class=\"muted small\">(Midterm Exams 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019 & 2020 (Compendium — Dr. Basheer Youssef))</span>",
          "a": "1. <b>Binary Search</b>: $\\Theta(\\lg n)$<br>2. <b>Insertion Sort</b>: $\\Theta(n^2)$<br>3. <b>Merge Sort</b>: $\\Theta(n \\lg n)$<br>4. <b>Counting Sort</b>: $\\Theta(n + k)$ (or $\\Theta(n)$ when $k = O(n)$)<br>5. <b>Heap Sort</b>: $\\Theta(n \\lg n)$<br>6. <b>Red-Black Tree (Search / Insert / Delete)</b>: $O(\\lg n)$."
        },
        {
          "q": "<b>Undated Midterm Q1</b>: Using Quicksort to sort the data <code>(5, 2, 4, 8, 0, 3, 1, 7)</code>, show the steps by drawing the array after each partition operation. <span class=\"muted small\">(Midterm Exams 2016, 2017, 2018, 2019 & 2020 (Compendium — Dr. Basheer Youssef))</span>",
          "a": "1. <b>Initial</b>: <code>[5, 2, 4, 8, 0, 3, 1, 7]</code> (pivot = <code>5</code>)<br>2. <b>After 1st Partition around 5</b>: <code>[1, 2, 4, 0, 3, | 5 |, 8, 7]</code> (pivot <code>5</code> at index 6)<br>3. <b>Partition Left <code>[1, 2, 4, 0, 3]</code> around 1</b>: <code>[0, | 1 |, 4, 2, 3]</code><br>4. <b>Partition <code>[4, 2, 3]</code> around 4</b>: <code>[3, 2, | 4 |]</code> $\\to$ partition <code>[3, 2]</code> around <code>3</code> gives <code>[2, 3, 4]</code><br>5. <b>Partition Right <code>[8, 7]</code> around 8</b>: <code>[7, | 8 |]</code><br>6. <b>Final Sorted Array</b>: <code>[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8]</code>."
        },
        {
          "q": "<b>Final 5 Q3 & Final 2016 Q3 (Eight Sorting Algorithms Column Matching)</b>: Match each intermediate column of the 24-word animal list (<code>deer, clam, bear, myna, tuna, slug, …</code>) to its sorting algorithm: <code>(0) Original input, (1) Sorted, (2) Selection sort, (3) Insertion sort, (4) Shellsort, (5) Mergesort, (6) Quicksort, (7) Heapsort</code>. <span class=\"muted small\">(Final Summer Exam & Workout Finals (2016, 2018 I/II, 2019 Credit & Exam Examples))</span>",
          "a": "<b>Official Column Order (Left to Right): <code>0, 4, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 1</code></b>:<br>• <b>Col 1 = (0) Original input</b>: <code>deer, clam, bear, myna, …</code><br>• <b>Col 2 = (4) Shellsort</b>: $h$-sorted subsequences (every 4th element is sorted).<br>• <b>Col 3 = (2) Selection sort</b>: First 13 elements are the global 13 smallest words in final sorted position (<code>bass, bull, bear…</code>) while the bottom suffix holds the remaining larger elements.<br>• <b>Col 4 = (3) Insertion sort</b>: First half of the array (`0..15`) is sorted relative to itself (`bass, bear, bull, calf, clam, crab, crow, deer, dove, duck, gnat, hoki, lion, lynx, myna, swan`), while `16..23` (`mule, hoki, duck, crab, crow, bass, lion, puma`) are untouched in their exact original positions!<br>• <b>Col 5 = (5) Mergesort</b>: Consists of sorted blocks of size 4/8 (`bear, bull, calf, clam, deer, dove, lynx, myna` | `slug, sole, tuna, …`).<br>• <b>Col 6 = (6) Quicksort</b>: Partitioned around pivot `deer` at index 7 (all elements `0..6` are $\\le$ `deer`, and all elements `8..23` are $\\ge$ `deer`).<br>• <b>Col 7 = (7) Heapsort</b>: Max-heap at top (`tuna, swan, sole, myna, lion…`) with largest sorted elements placed at the bottom."
        },
        {
          "q": "<b>Final 1 Q1 & Final 2016 Q6 / Final 5 Q7</b>: Find the shortest paths from node <code>s</code> to every node (<code>2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, t</code>) in the 8-node directed graph (with edges <code>s→2:9, s→6:14, s→7:15, 2→3:24, 6→3:18, 6→5:30, 6→7:5, 7→5:20, 7→t:44, 5→3:2, 5→4:11, 5→t:16, 4→3:6, 4→t:6, 3→t:19</code>) using Dijkstra's Algorithm. <span class=\"muted small\">(Final Summer Exam & Workout Finals (2016, 2018 I/II, 2019 Credit & Exam Examples))</span>",
          "a": "<b>Step-by-Step Dijkstra Settlement from <code>s</code></b>:<br>1. <b>Init at <code>s</code></b>: $d[s]=0$. Relax out of $s$: $d[2]=9(p=s),\\; d[6]=14(p=s),\\; d[7]=15(p=s)$.<br>2. <b>Extract <code>2</code> ($d[2]=9$)</b>: Relax $2\\to 3$: $d[3]=9+24=33(p=2)$.<br>3. <b>Extract <code>6</code> ($d[6]=14$)</b>: Relax $6\\to 3$: $d[3]=\\min(33, 14+18)=32(p=6)$; $6\\to 5$: $d[5]=14+30=44(p=6)$; $6\\to 7$: $14+5=19 > 15$.<br>4. <b>Extract <code>7</code> ($d[7]=15$)</b>: Relax $7\\to 5$: $d[5]=\\min(44, 15+20)=35(p=7)$; $7\\to t$: $d[t]=15+44=59(p=7)$.<br>5. <b>Extract <code>3</code> ($d[3]=32$)</b>: Relax $3\\to t$: $d[t]=\\min(59, 32+19)=51(p=3)$.<br>6. <b>Extract <code>5</code> ($d[5]=35$)</b>: Relax $5\\to 3$: $35+2=37 > 32$; $5\\to 4$: $d[4]=35+11=46(p=5)$; $5\\to t$: $d[t]=\\min(51, 35+16)=51(p=3/5)$.<br>7. <b>Extract <code>4</code> ($d[4]=46$)</b>: Relax $4\\to t$: $46+6=52 > 51$.<br>8. <b>Extract <code>t</code> ($d[t]=51$)</b>.<br><b>Final Distances & Predecessors</b>: <code>2: 9(s), 6: 14(s), 7: 15(s), 3: 32(6), 5: 35(7), 4: 46(5), t: 51(3 or 5)</code>.<br><b>Shortest Path $s \\leadsto t$</b>: <code>s → 6 → 3 → t</code> ($14 + 18 + 19 = \\mathbf{51}$) or <code>s → 7 → 5 → t</code> ($15 + 20 + 16 = \\mathbf{51}$)."
        }
