            "<b>Big-Oh Definition & Proofs (Slides 2.40–2.43)</b>: $f(n) \\in O(g(n)) \\iff \\exists c > 0, n_0 > 0$ such that $f(n) \\le c g(n)$ for all $n \\ge n_0$.<ul><li><b>Prove $3n + 2 \\in O(n)$</b>: $3n + 2 \\le 5n$ for all $n \\ge 1$ ($c=5, n_0=1$).</li><li><b>Prove $3n^2 + 20 \\in O(n^2)$</b>: $3n^2 + 20 \\le 4n^2 \\iff n^2 \\ge 20 \\iff n \\ge 5$ ($c=4, n_0=5$).</li></ul>",
            "<b>Big-Oh Rules (Slides 2.47–2.52)</b>:<ul><li><b>Rule #1 (Sum Rule — Sequential steps)</b>: $O(f(n)) + O(g(n)) = O(f(n) + g(n)) = O(\\max(f(n), g(n)))$.</li><li><b>Rule #2 (Product Rule — Nested loops)</b>: $O(f(n)) \\cdot O(g(n)) = O(f(n) \\cdot g(n))$.</li></ul>",
            "<b>Comparing 3 Summation Algorithms for $1 + 2 + \\dots + n$ (Slides 2.56–2.59)</b>: Algorithm A (single loop): $n$ additions $= \\Theta(n)$; Algorithm B (nested loop): $n(n+1)/2$ additions $= \\Theta(n^2)$; Algorithm C ($n(n+1)/2$ formula): $3$ operations $= \\Theta(1)$.",
            "<b>$\\Omega$ and $\\Theta$ (Slides 2.60–2.61)</b>: $\\Omega(g(n))$ is the asymptotic lower bound ($f(n) \\ge c g(n)$), and $\\Theta(g(n))$ is the tight bound ($f(n) \\in O(g(n))$ and $f(n) \\in \\Omega(g(n))$)."
