              "q": "<b>Q1 (10 points)</b>: What do the following functions <code>F2(A, l, mid, r)</code> and <code>F1(A, l, r)</code> do, what is the recurrence equation for the <code>F1</code> function, and what is its time complexity?<br><pre>F2(A, l, mid, r):\n  left_sum = -∞; sum = 0\n  for i from mid down to l:\n    sum = sum + A[i]\n    if sum > left_sum: left_sum = sum\n  right_sum = -∞; sum = 0\n  for i from mid+1 up to r:\n    sum = sum + A[i]\n    if sum > right_sum: right_sum = sum\n  return left_sum + right_sum\n\nF1(A, l, r):\n  if l == r: return A[l]\n  mid = (l + r) // 2\n  left_best = F1(A, l, mid)\n  right_best = F1(A, mid+1, r)\n  cross_best = F2(A, l, mid, r)\n  return max(left_best, right_best, cross_best)</pre>",
              "ans": "1. <b>What <code>F2(A, l, mid, r)</code> does</b>: Finds the maximum sum of any contiguous subarray of <code>A[l..r]</code> that <b>crosses the midpoint <code>mid</code></b> (by scanning left from <code>mid</code> down to <code>l</code> and right from <code>mid+1</code> up to <code>r</code> in $\\Theta(n)$ linear time, where $n = r - l + 1$).<br>2. <b>What <code>F1(A, l, r)</code> does</b>: Solves the <b>Maximum Contiguous Subarray Problem</b> on <code>A[l..r]</code> using Divide-and-Conquer (returning the maximum sum among all contiguous subarrays in the left half, right half, or crossing the midpoint).<br>3. <b>Recurrence Equation for <code>F1</code></b>: $T(n) = 2T(n/2) + \\Theta(n)$ for $n > 1$, with base case $T(1) = \\Theta(1)$.<br>4. <b>Time Complexity</b>: By Master Theorem Case 2 ($a=2, b=2, n^{\\log_2 2} = n = \\Theta(f(n))$), $T(n) = \\mathbf{\\Theta(n \\lg n)}$.",
