              "q": "<b>Q3 (5 points)</b>: Prove that a Red-Black Tree with $n$ keys has height $h \\le 2 \\lg(n + 1)$.",
              "ans": "1. <b>Subtree Size Lemma</b>: Prove by induction on height that the subtree rooted at any node $x$ contains at least $2^{bh(x)} - 1$ internal nodes. Base case: If $x = \\text{NIL}$, $bh(x) = 0$ and $2^0 - 1 = 0$. Inductive step: For an internal node $x$, each child has black-height $\\ge bh(x) - 1$, so by the inductive hypothesis each child's subtree has $\\ge 2^{bh(x)-1} - 1$ internal nodes; thus $x$'s subtree has $\\ge 2(2^{bh(x)-1} - 1) + 1 = 2^{bh(x)} - 1$ internal nodes.<br>2. <b>Relating $bh(\\text{root})$ to $h$</b>: By Property 3 (no two consecutive red nodes on any path), at least half of the nodes on any root-to-leaf path are black, so $bh(\\text{root}) \\ge h / 2$.<br>3. <b>Conclusion</b>: $n \\ge 2^{bh(\\text{root})} - 1 \\ge 2^{h/2} - 1 \\implies n + 1 \\ge 2^{h/2} \\implies \\lg(n + 1) \\ge h / 2 \\implies \\mathbf{h \\le 2 \\lg(n + 1)}$.",
              "why": "Merging red nodes into their black parents yields a 2-3-4 tree of depth h' ≥ h/2 with n + 1 ≥ 2^(h') leaves."
