              "q": "<b>Q1 (10 points)</b>: Suppose you are given a source string $S[0..n-1]$ over $\\{a, b\\}$ and a pattern string $P[0..m-1]$ ($m \\ll n$) over $\\{a, b, *\\}$, where <code>*</code> is a wildcard matching either $a$ or $b$. Output a sorted list $M$ of valid match positions $j$ where $P$ matches $S[j..j+m-1]$ (e.g., for $S = \\text{ababbab}$ and $P = \\text{ab*}$, $M = [0, 2]$). Describe a straightforward naive algorithm running in $O(nm)$ time.",
              "ans": "<b>Naive $O(nm)$ Wildcard Pattern Matching Algorithm</b>:<br><pre>vector&lt;int&gt; findMatches(string S, string P) {\n  int n = S.length(), m = P.length();\n  vector&lt;int&gt; M;\n  for (int j = 0; j &lt;= n - m; j++) {\n    bool match = true;\n    for (int k = 0; k &lt; m; k++) {\n      if (P[k] != '*' &amp;&amp; P[k] != S[j + k]) {\n        match = false;\n        break;\n      }\n    }\n    if (match) M.push_back(j);\n  }\n  return M; // Naturally sorted in increasing order of j\n}</pre><b>Complexity</b>: Outer loop runs $n - m + 1 = O(n)$ times; inner loop runs at most $m$ comparisons per shift $j \\implies \\mathbf{O(nm)}$ time.",
